+## D1 — Construct exact counts and negative predicate witnesses (review-7444e31d07487847)
+
+**Observed defect.** The inherited sampler mostly emitted firing four-corner
+rectangles, accepted several inert/unknown controls, and could exhaust retries
+then silently return fewer objects than requested. A learner could therefore
+fit the lesson with “fill every nonzero bounding box” without learning the
+exact-four-corners predicate.
+
+**Repair.** Version 4.0.0 uses role-aware disjoint zones. It constructively
+places exactly 1–3 target rectangles and 1–2 distractors on every declared
+7×8 through 18×22 canvas, with no retry loop or count fallback. Every target is
+at least 3×3, so the filled answer has a genuine interior. The default
+three-sample schedule cycles through three-corners, four-corners-plus-extra,
+and misplaced-four negatives. Unknown and removed controls fail visibly.
+
+**Declared competitors.** The executable rival set covers filling every box,
+requiring merely four cells, requiring all corners while ignoring extra cells,
+preserving nonqualifiers, and drawing only outlines. The ordinary three-example
+curriculum defeats each declared competitor.
+
+**Verification.** The focused suite passes 28 tests, including exact joint
+boundary counts, deterministic sampling, canonical mirror parity, schedule
+coverage, and the 7×8 / three-target / two-distractor boundary. The reusable
+`workspace/adversarial_review.py` replay checked 27 generated cases and five
+canonical pairs in 32 protected-Racket evaluations; all five declared rivals
+were defeated. Lint, self-check, rule smoke, override, semantic, and authority
+synchronization gates also passed on the final source.
+
+**Status and limit.** This is a reviewer-built repair proposal for a catalog-
+unapproved target. It is finite declared-rival evidence, not owner acceptance,
+canonical approval, or training admission.
